From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: mhi_net: refill the DL ring in-line from the rx callback

mhi_net only refills the DL ring from a delayed work, and only once the ring
has drained to half (free_desc_count >= rx_queue_sz / 2). Under sustained
downlink on a slow, busy SoC (IPQ5018, dual A53) the remaining half is consumed
by the modem in a few milliseconds - faster than system_wq can schedule the
worker - so the ring hits empty. A hardware-accelerated modem DL engine
(Qualcomm SDX IPA on the RG520N) then deadlocks on descriptor starvation and
never resumes: rx freezes, tx keeps flowing, link stays up, only a modem reset
recovers it.

Every other high-rate Linux NIC refills its RX ring in-line from the receive
path, not from a workqueue. Do the same: for each buffer the device just
consumed, hand one back immediately from the DL callback so the ring never runs
dry. This is safe - the MHI host drops mhi_chan->lock around the transfer
callback (parse_xfer_event) and the callback runs in softirq, so
netdev_alloc_skb()/mhi_queue_skb() can be called directly. The delayed work is
kept as the fallback for the rare allocation/queue failure, as the vendor
driver does.

Error and teardown behaviour, audited rather than assumed:

 - The channel teardown path cannot reach this code. mhi_reset_data_chan()
   returns outstanding buffers with result.transaction_status = -ENOTCONN, and
   mhi_net_dl_callback() handles -ENOTCONN by freeing the skb and returning
   before the refill, so nothing here runs while the channel is being reset and
   the work is never scheduled from that path.
 - mhi_process_data_event_ring() does read_unlock_bh(&mhi_chan->lock) before
   invoking the callback, so queueing from inside it takes no lock that is
   already held.
 - netdev_alloc_skb() allocates with GFP_ATOMIC, which is what softirq context
   requires.
 - The mhi_queue_is_full() test can race the fallback worker. That is benign:
   mhi_queue_skb() remains the authoritative check and the newly allocated skb
   is freed if it refuses.
 - mhi_ndo_stop() does cancel_delayed_work_sync() on the refill work. A
   concurrent dl_callback can still re-schedule it after that cancellation;
   that window predates this patch, which only changes when the work is used as
   a fallback rather than as the normal path.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/mhi_net.c
+++ b/drivers/net/mhi_net.c
@@ -171,9 +171,6 @@
 {
 	struct mhi_net_dev *mhi_netdev = dev_get_drvdata(&mhi_dev->dev);
 	struct sk_buff *skb = mhi_res->buf_addr;
-	int free_desc_count;
-
-	free_desc_count = mhi_get_free_desc_count(mhi_dev, DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
 
 	if (unlikely(mhi_res->transaction_status)) {
 		switch (mhi_res->transaction_status) {
@@ -228,9 +225,34 @@
 		__netif_rx(skb);
 	}
 
-	/* Refill if RX buffers queue becomes low */
-	if (free_desc_count >= mhi_netdev->rx_queue_sz / 2)
-		schedule_delayed_work(&mhi_netdev->rx_refill, 0);
+	/* Refill in-line: hand one buffer back for every one the device just
+	 * consumed, so the ring is replenished at the rate it is drained rather
+	 * than whenever a worker next gets to run. Deferring to the workqueue
+	 * let the ring empty under sustained DL faster than the worker was
+	 * scheduled, starving - and then permanently wedging - the modem's
+	 * hardware DL engine. This does not guarantee the ring never drains: an
+	 * allocation failure still falls back to the work, and the device can
+	 * still outrun the host.
+	 *
+	 * Safe here because mhi_process_data_event_ring() drops mhi_chan->lock
+	 * before invoking this callback, netdev_alloc_skb() allocates with
+	 * GFP_ATOMIC, and we run in softirq. mhi_queue_skb() - not the
+	 * queue_is_full() test above - is the authoritative check, so losing
+	 * that race against the fallback worker only costs one allocation.
+	 */
+	if (!mhi_queue_is_full(mhi_dev, DMA_FROM_DEVICE)) {
+		unsigned int size = mhi_netdev->mru ? mhi_netdev->mru :
+					READ_ONCE(mhi_netdev->ndev->mtu);
+		struct sk_buff *nskb = netdev_alloc_skb(mhi_netdev->ndev, size);
+
+		if (unlikely(!nskb)) {
+			schedule_delayed_work(&mhi_netdev->rx_refill, 0);
+		} else if (unlikely(mhi_queue_skb(mhi_dev, DMA_FROM_DEVICE, nskb,
+						  size, MHI_EOT))) {
+			kfree_skb(nskb);
+			schedule_delayed_work(&mhi_netdev->rx_refill, 0);
+		}
+	}
 }
 
 static void mhi_net_ul_callback(struct mhi_device *mhi_dev,
